# Skia Canvas Backend for ESP32-S3 [SPEC:CXC-CYD-SKIA-S3]

> The custom `canvas_esp32.cpp` backend exists because the CYD's ESP32-D0WD has 520KB of SRAM, no PSRAM,
> and 4MB of flash. Every one of its techniques — integer arithmetic, SDF anti-aliasing, band rendering
> — is a concession to that budget, and each concession costs pixel fidelity against the desktop. An
> ESP32-S3 with PSRAM has none of those constraints: a full framebuffer fits, and Skia's own CPU
> rasteriser fits in flash. This spec takes the obvious consequence seriously — on that class of part,
> run the SAME rasteriser as the desktop and the geometric divergence does not need to be minimised
> because it does not exist. The result is one rendering engine and TWO classes of embedded target,
> selected at build time behind an unchanged Canvas interface.

---

## 1. Rationale

### [CXC-CYD-SKIA-RATIONALE] PSRAM is what makes the tradeoff unnecessary rather than merely cheaper

- premise: `canvas_esp32.cpp` was designed for the ESP32-D0WD (CYD) — 520KB SRAM, no PSRAM, 4MB flash.
  It uses integer arithmetic, SDF-based AA and band rendering to fit those constraints.
- cost: those tradeoffs produce visible pixel-level divergence from the desktop Skia backend —
  staircased corners, different alpha blending, and coarser anti-aliasing.

| Resource | ESP32 (CYD) | ESP32-S3 | ESP32-P4 |
|----------|-------------|----------|----------|
| SRAM | 520KB | 512KB | 768KB |
| PSRAM | None | 2-8MB (QSPI/OPI) | 2-32MB (OPI) |
| Flash | 4MB | 4-16MB | 4-16MB |
| CPU | Dual 240MHz | Dual 240MHz + vector instructions | Single 400MHz RISC-V + LP core |
| Display interface | SPI only | SPI, I2C, parallel 8/16-bit, LCD-CAM | MIPI-DSI, parallel RGB888, LCD-CAM |

- arithmetic: with 2-8MB PSRAM a full 240x320 RGB8888 framebuffer (307KB), or even 480x320 (614KB),
  fits comfortably, and the Skia CPU rasteriser binary (1-2MB) fits in flash.
- consequence: the Skia approach becomes viable and eliminates ALL geometric rendering divergence,
  rather than reducing it.
- larger-displays: for 800x480 and above the ESP32-P4 is the stronger target — its MIPI-DSI interface
  drives high-resolution IPS/OLED panels directly without SPI bandwidth limits, 32MB PSRAM accommodates
  an 800x480 RGBA8888 framebuffer (1.5MB) plus Skia internals and double buffering, and the 400MHz
  RISC-V core provides roughly 1.7x the single-thread throughput of the ESP32-S3.
- portability: the SAME `SkiaEmbeddedCanvas` backend targets both parts; only the display driver and the
  PlatformIO board configuration differ.
- part-of: [SPEC:CXC-CYD-SKIA-S3]

---

## 2. Architecture

### [CXC-CYD-SKIA-ARCH-SELECT] Two backends coexist and are selected at compile time

- definition: two C++ Canvas backends live in the firmware source and are chosen by build flag, not at
  runtime.

| Backend | Target | File | Selection |
|---------|--------|------|-----------|
| `ESP32Canvas` | ESP32 (no PSRAM) | `canvas_esp32.cpp` | `#if !CONFIG_SPIRAM` or build flag |
| `SkiaEmbeddedCanvas` | ESP32-S3 (PSRAM) | `canvas_skia_embedded.cpp` | `#if CONFIG_SPIRAM` or build flag |

- contract: both implement the SAME `Canvas` abstract interface from `canvas.h`.
- consequence: widget code, the main loop and touch dispatch are ALL unchanged. Only the rendering
  backend differs, which is what keeps this spec from touching anything above the canvas.
- part-of: [SPEC:CXC-CYD-SKIA-S3]

### [CXC-CYD-SKIA-ARCH-PIPELINE] The rendering pipeline from widget call to panel

- definition: `Widget.draw(canvas, rect)` issues Canvas drawing commands (`fill_rect`, `fill_arc`,
  `draw_text` and the rest); `SkiaEmbeddedCanvas` translates each to a Skia API call; Skia's CPU
  rasteriser renders into an `SkBitmap` (RGB8888 in PSRAM); and `present()` converts RGB8888 to RGB565
  and DMA-transfers it to the display.
- rule: the translation layer is the ONLY new code in the path. Rasterisation is Skia's, and the
  transfer is the display driver's.
- part-of: [SPEC:CXC-CYD-SKIA-S3]

### [CXC-CYD-SKIA-ARCH-MEMORY] The PSRAM budget, and the band option for larger panels

- definition: the buffers this backend needs and where each one lives.

| Buffer | Size (240x320) | Size (480x320) | Location |
|--------|---------------|-----------------|----------|
| Skia SkBitmap (RGBA8888) | 307KB | 614KB | PSRAM |
| Display transfer buffer (RGB565) | 150KB or band | 300KB or band | PSRAM or SRAM |
| Skia internal allocator | ~200-500KB | ~200-500KB | PSRAM |
| Widget tree and app logic | ~10-50KB | ~10-50KB | SRAM |

- total: roughly 700KB to 1MB of PSRAM for 240x320 — well within the 2MB minimum.
- band-option: for 480x320 or 320x480, band rendering can be used on the SKIA side too — render to a
  Skia surface sized to the band, convert, DMA, advance. This caps PSRAM usage at
  `band_height * width * 4` bytes per band.
- consequence: band rendering survives as an OPTION on this tier rather than a requirement, which is the
  precise difference between the two backends' relationship to memory.
- part-of: [SPEC:CXC-CYD-SKIA-S3]

---

## 3. Skia integration

### [CXC-CYD-SKIA-BUILD-CONFIG] The Skia GN configuration, and what each flag buys

- definition: Skia supports embedded targets through its GN build system; this is the required flag set.

| Flag | Value | Rationale |
|------|-------|-----------|
| `skia_use_gl` | false | No GPU — CPU rasteriser only |
| `skia_use_vulkan` | false | No GPU |
| `skia_enable_gpu` | false | Saves ~500KB |
| `skia_use_freetype` | true | Font rendering — use the bitmap atlas instead if too large |
| `skia_use_harfbuzz` | false | No complex text shaping needed |
| `skia_use_icu` | false | No internationalisation |
| `skia_use_libjpeg_turbo_decode` | false | PNG alone is sufficient |
| `skia_use_libpng_decode` | true | PNG asset loading |
| `skia_use_zlib` | true | Required by libpng |
| `target_cpu` | "arm" | ESP32-S3 is Xtensa LX7 and needs a custom toolchain config |

- size-estimate: 1-2MB for the CPU rasteriser plus basic font and PNG decode, which fits in 4MB flash
  with room for app code and the font atlas.
- toolchain-risk: the ESP32-S3 is Xtensa LX7, NOT ARM. Skia's build system needs a custom toolchain
  definition pointing at the ESP-IDF Xtensa compiler. This is the primary integration challenge — Skia
  has been built for ARM embedded targets but not for Xtensa. See [CXC-CYD-SKIA-RISK-XTENSA].
- part-of: [SPEC:CXC-CYD-SKIA-S3]

### [CXC-CYD-SKIA-CANVAS] SkiaEmbeddedCanvas is a near-1:1 wrapper over the Skia C++ API

- file: cyd/src/canvas_skia_embedded.cpp
- definition: a `Canvas` subclass that wraps Skia's C++ API; each Canvas method maps to a single Skia
  call.
- method-create-surface: `create_surface(int w, int h)` allocates the `SkBitmap` in PSRAM via
  `allocPixels(SkImageInfo::MakeN32Premul(w, h))` and constructs the `SkCanvas` over it.
- method-fill-rect: `fill_rect(x, y, w, h)` maps to `drawRect(SkRect::MakeXYWH(...), _fill_paint)`.
- method-fill-circle: `fill_circle(cx, cy, r)` maps to `drawCircle(cx, cy, r, _fill_paint)`.
- method-fill-round-rect: `fill_round_rect(x, y, w, h, radius)` maps to `drawRoundRect(...)`.
- method-draw-text: `draw_text(text, x, y, align)` uses either the bitmap font atlas (for cross-tier
  fidelity) or Skia native text (for maximum quality) per [CXC-CYD-SKIA-FONT].
- method-present: `present()` converts RGBA8888 to RGB565 and DMA-transfers via
  `_display->transfer_framebuffer(...)`.
- state: the class holds an `SkBitmap` (PSRAM-allocated), a `std::unique_ptr<SkCanvas>`, an `SkPaint`
  for fill and one for stroke, and an `IDisplay*`.
- size-estimate: the implementation should be roughly 200-300 lines, because the API mapping is nearly
  one-to-one.
- part-of: [SPEC:CXC-CYD-SKIA-S3]

### [CXC-CYD-SKIA-FONT] Text has two modes, and the choice is a fidelity-versus-quality trade

- definition: two font strategies, selectable at build time.

| Option | Approach | Fidelity target |
|--------|----------|----------------|
| Atlas mode | The same bitmap font atlas as ESP32Canvas and desktop atlas mode | Pixel-identical to the CYD and to desktop-with-atlas |
| Skia native | Skia's built-in FreeType text rendering | Pixel-identical to desktop-native, highest quality |
- rule: for cross-platform visual QA — pixel comparison against the CYD — use ATLAS mode. For production
  displays where quality matters more than CYD matching, use Skia native.
- consequence: the two modes are not interchangeable at comparison time. A pixel comparison against the
  CYD is only meaningful in atlas mode, and reporting a native-mode delta against the CYD measures the
  mode choice rather than the backend.
- part-of: [SPEC:CXC-CYD-SKIA-S3]

---

## 4. Display abstraction

### [CXC-CYD-SKIA-DISPLAY-IFACE] IDisplay decouples the backend from the panel controller

- definition: an abstract interface behind which any display controller can sit, so the Canvas backend
  is not written against ILI9341.
- api: `virtual void init()`; `virtual void transfer_framebuffer(const uint16_t* buf, uint16_t y_start,
  uint16_t y_end)`; `virtual void transfer_band(const uint16_t* buf, uint16_t y_start, uint16_t y_end)`;
  `virtual uint16_t width() const`; `virtual uint16_t height() const`.
- existing: the current `ILI9341` class implements this for the CYD; new drivers implement the same
  interface.

| Display | Controller | Interface | Resolution | Notes |
|---------|-----------|-----------|------------|-------|
| CYD TFT | ILI9341 | SPI | 240x320 | Existing driver |
| OLED | SSD1351 / SH1107 | SPI / I2C | 128x128 to 256x64 | High contrast, true black |
| IPS TFT | ST7789 / ILI9488 | SPI / parallel | 240x320 to 480x320 | Wide viewing angle |
| Round OLED | GC9A01 | SPI | 240x240 | Circular gauge displays |

- rationale: both a full-framebuffer and a band transfer are on the interface because the two are
  genuinely different operations on this tier — the framebuffer path is the normal one and the band path
  is the large-panel fallback from [CXC-CYD-SKIA-ARCH-MEMORY].
- part-of: [SPEC:CXC-CYD-SKIA-S3]

### [CXC-CYD-SKIA-DISPLAY-CONVERT] The RGBA8888 to RGB565 conversion is the ONLY quantisation left

- definition: Skia renders RGBA8888 and most displays accept RGB565, so `present()` converts by
  extracting each channel and truncating — `((r >> 3) << 11) | ((g >> 2) << 5) | (b >> 3)`.
- claim: this conversion is the ONLY remaining source of colour quantisation in the whole pipeline, at
  plus or minus 3 per channel.
- consequence: the pixel comparison tool already accounts for RGB565 tolerance, so once geometric
  divergence is gone the expected residual is exactly this and nothing else.
- optimisation: on displays that accept RGB888 (some OLED controllers), skip the conversion entirely and
  pass Skia's buffer straight through — which removes the last quantisation as well.
- part-of: [SPEC:CXC-CYD-SKIA-S3]

---

## 5. Build system

### [CXC-CYD-SKIA-BUILD-PIO] A new PlatformIO environment alongside the CYD one

- definition: environment `[env:esp32s3_skia]` on platform `espressif32`, board `esp32-s3-devkitc-1`,
  framework `espidf`.
- cmake-args: `-DUSE_SKIA_BACKEND=ON` and `-DSKIA_DIR=${PROJECT_DIR}/lib/skia` passed through
  `board_build.cmake_extra_args`.
- constraint: the existing CYD environment is untouched and must keep building, which SS-C6 verifies.
- part-of: [SPEC:CXC-CYD-SKIA-S3]

### [CXC-CYD-SKIA-BUILD-COMPONENT] Skia ships as a PRE-BUILT ESP-IDF component

- definition: Skia is pre-built as a static library for Xtensa LX7 and included as an ESP-IDF component
  at `cyd/components/skia/` — a `CMakeLists.txt` component wrapper, an `include/` subset of Skia
  headers, and `lib/libskia.a` built for `xtensa-esp32s3-elf`.
- rationale: pre-building avoids compiling Skia from source on every firmware build, which takes about
  30 minutes.
- provenance: the pre-built library is either committed or fetched by a build script.
- risk: a committed binary blob is opaque to review and pins a Skia version implicitly; whichever route
  is taken, the Skia revision the library was built from must be recorded alongside it.
- part-of: [SPEC:CXC-CYD-SKIA-S3]

### [CXC-CYD-SKIA-BUILD-CONDITIONAL] A canvas_factory header picks the type at compile time

- file: cyd/include/canvas_factory.h
- definition: when `USE_SKIA_BACKEND` is defined AND `CONFIG_SPIRAM` is set, `PlatformCanvas` aliases
  `SkiaEmbeddedCanvas`; otherwise it aliases `ESP32Canvas`.
- consequence: `main.cpp` refers only to `PlatformCanvas` — the same source, a different backend
  selected at build time, with no runtime branch and no virtual dispatch cost at the selection point.
- part-of: [SPEC:CXC-CYD-SKIA-S3]

---

## 6. Validation

### [CXC-CYD-SKIA-VALIDATION-TARGET] The convergence this backend is expected to produce

- definition: the before-and-after comparison figures that justify the work.

| Comparison | Before (custom renderer) | After (Skia on S3) |
|-----------|-------------------------|-------------------|
| Desktop-atlas vs ESP32 | 55% near-match, max delta 136 | >99% near-match (RGB565 quantisation only) |
| Geometric AA divergence | Visible on all curves | Eliminated — the same Skia rasteriser |
| Text divergence (with atlas) | Near-zero | Near-zero (unchanged) |

- claim: the only remaining divergence should be RGB565 colour quantisation at plus or minus 3 per
  channel, which the pixel comparison tool already accounts for.
- invalidating-assumption: that Skia's CPU rasteriser is deterministic across architectures at the byte
  level. If Xtensa float behaviour or a portable-C++ code path differs from the desktop's SIMD path, the
  >99% target is not reachable and the residual must be re-characterised before it is called a defect.
- part-of: [SPEC:CXC-CYD-SKIA-S3]

### [CXC-CYD-SKIA-VALIDATION-QA] Automated QA reuses the CYD capture pipeline unchanged

- definition: the same pipeline as the CYD — the ESP32-S3 renders the dashboard with Skia, captures a
  BMP and pushes it to document_store; the desktop renders the same dashboard with Skia to PNG;
  `pixel_comparison.py` compares them; and screenshots are saved to `cyd/screenshots/progression/` with
  a device tag.
- reuse: the capture and push half is [SPEC:CXC-CYD-CAPTURE] unchanged; only the device tag is new.
- verify-machine: `pixel_comparison.py` reports greater than 99% near-match between the ESP32-S3 capture
  and the desktop render.
- verify-human: the S3 panel and the desktop render show the same curves and corners; a machine-green
  near-match with visibly different curve edges means the comparison tolerance is absorbing the very
  divergence this spec exists to remove.
- part-of: [SPEC:CXC-CYD-SKIA-S3]

---

## 7. Phasing, risk and acceptance

### [CXC-CYD-SKIA-PHASES] The six delivery phases

- definition: the ordered phase plan, each with its deliverable.

| Phase | Scope | Deliverable |
|-------|-------|-------------|
| S3-1 | Skia cross-compilation | `libskia.a` built for xtensa-esp32s3-elf, basic drawing test |
| S3-2 | SkiaEmbeddedCanvas implementation | All Canvas methods mapped to Skia, rendering to a PSRAM bitmap |
| S3-3 | Display driver abstraction | `IDisplay` interface, ST7789/SSD1351 drivers alongside ILI9341 |
| S3-4 | present() pipeline | RGBA8888 to RGB565 conversion, DMA transfer, band rendering option |
| S3-5 | Font atlas integration | Bitmap atlas text rendering via a Skia surface, matching CYD output |
| S3-6 | Pixel comparison validation | Automated QA showing >99% near-match against the desktop |

- gating: S3-1 gates everything. If Skia cannot be cross-compiled for Xtensa the rest of the plan does
  not begin, which is why [CXC-CYD-SKIA-RISK-XTENSA] is the first thing to settle.
- part-of: [SPEC:CXC-CYD-SKIA-S3]

### [CXC-CYD-SKIA-RISK-XTENSA] RISK — Skia's GN build does not support Xtensa

- risk: Skia's build system (GN) does not natively support Xtensa, and the ESP32-S3 is Xtensa LX7.
- approach-cmake: build the Skia source files directly through ESP-IDF's CMake, bypassing GN. Requires
  enumerating source files manually but avoids toolchain configuration. Community projects such as
  esp-idf-skia may exist.
- approach-gn: add an Xtensa toolchain definition to Skia's GN configs. More complex but more
  maintainable.
- approach-portable: disable all SIMD and platform-specific code paths and build the portable C++ only.
  Slower, but guaranteed to compile on any C++17 toolchain including Xtensa.
- recommendation: start with the PORTABLE C++ approach to validate feasibility, then optimise with the
  CMake wrapper for build integration. Feasibility first, performance second — the risk being retired
  is "can this be built at all", not "is it fast".
- consequence: the portable path forgoes SIMD, so the S3-6 frame-time target is measured against the
  slowest viable configuration and can only improve.
- part-of: [SPEC:CXC-CYD-SKIA-S3]

### [CXC-CYD-SKIA-COMPLIANCE] The compliance contract

- definition: eight acceptance criteria spanning interface completeness, pixel convergence, memory,
  timing, flash, non-regression, display abstraction and the band option.

| ID | Requirement | Verification |
|----|-------------|-------------|
| SS-C1 | SkiaEmbeddedCanvas implements every Canvas interface method | Compiles without pure virtual errors; renders the test dashboard |
| SS-C2 | The same dashboard renders identically on desktop Skia and ESP32-S3 Skia | pixel_comparison.py: >99% near-match at RGB565 tolerance |
| SS-C3 | PSRAM allocation succeeds for the framebuffer plus Skia internals | Boot log shows successful allocation and a heap report |
| SS-C4 | Frame rendering completes within an acceptable time | Under 500ms for a full 240x320 frame; target under 200ms |
| SS-C5 | Firmware builds with Skia included | Flash usage under 80% of 4MB |
| SS-C6 | canvas_esp32.cpp is unchanged and still builds for ESP32 targets | `pio run -e cyd` succeeds with no modifications |
| SS-C7 | The display abstraction supports ILI9341 and at least one new controller | Two drivers implement IDisplay and both render correctly |
| SS-C8 | The band rendering option works on the Skia backend | For displays larger than 240x320, PSRAM-efficient band rendering produces correct output |

- part-of: [SPEC:CXC-CYD-SKIA-S3]

